this morning we're going to begin with a  look into the allegations into the inner  workings of the Church of Scientology  they come in a new book it's called a  billion years Mike rinder details the  the decades he spent as a high-ranking  Scientology member the book is published  by an imprint of Simon and Schuster  that's a division of our parent company  Paramount Global in a wide-ranging  interview rinder told Chief  investigative correspondent Jim Axelrod  about his experience as a high up  official in the organization Jim also  spoke to a Scientology representative  who disputes many of rinder's claims and  calls him an unreliable source and a  quote inveterate liar Jim good morning  good morning founded in the early 1950s  with a remarkable origin story involving  Galactic Warlords rocket ships and 75  million year old volcanoes Scientology  State church leaders is a belief system  designed to Foster spiritual  enlightenment Mike rinder grew up in the  church but he told me he went from  wanting to save the world with  Scientology to wanting to save the world  from Scientology  Mike how you doing Mike rinder is no fan  of Scientology please welcome Mr Mike  rinder  which is remarkable since he spent 46  years of his life in the church rising  to a high-ranking position I told a lot  of lies when I was the spokesperson for  Scientology his Memoir a billion years  describes an organization built on the  teachings of one man scientology's  founder L Ron Hubbard We Believe The  elrond Habit had the answers to  everything about life and L Ron Hubbard  based his General view of how to fix all  problems facing mankind what did he  ground it in  but  Hubbard who'd been a science fiction  writer recorded 3 000 lectures on how to  handle every conceivable challenge a  Scientologist could face which he  blended with some outrageous claims El  Ron Hubbard says for instance smoking  doesn't cause cancer yeah not smoking  enough causes cancer right but that's  sort of insanity  is also indicative of just how powerful  the grip on people's minds is  scientology's journey to spiritual  enlightenment is done through a series  of courses that rinder says costs money  a lot of money three hundred five  hundred thousand that's for you to pay  for your own participation in  Scientology this is why I said it's a  business masquerading as a religion does  it cost 300 400 500 000 to reach  spiritual enlightenment no absolutely  not for more than three decades Monique  Yingling who emphasized that she's a  practicing Catholic has been a lawyer  for the church the total contributions  that you would be asked for if you did  every single course on the training  route would be less than fifty thousand  dollars closer to 20 or 30 or something  like that I can assure you we are not  what you expect in 1987 David miscavige  took over as leader of the church  following Hubbard's death rinder left  the church 20 years later after he says  he he was punished by Miss cabbage  following a news account critical of  Scientology rinder says Miss cabbage  sent him to the hole a Detention Center  on the grounds of scientology's  headquarters in Southern California it  consisted of two double wide trailers  that have been put on foundations and  connected and it was how many people  might be in the hall at one time well I  counted at one point that there was a  hundred and thirty 130 and two double  wides yes  and the doors were bad there was  bathrooms but no showers uh the sleeping  Arrangements were on the floor there's  no such thing as the whole it's it is a  startling  if not stunning description  why would he lie about this because it's  salacious and what draws the media's  attention is throwing in all these  salacious details that are always  categorically untrue  not always categorically untrue I'm not  saying that and I you know and I think  there's always a grain of Truth to  everything myself so as someone who's  been around the church for 35 years  what's the granted truth here the grain  of Truth is that there the church does  have a system of internal discipline  when  um that individuals and for the most  part individuals volunteer for it and  they say they're just very grateful that  they had the opportunity first of all to  redeem themselves and to study more  fully the scriptures of Scientology  rinder says leaving the church was one  thing but untangling himself from a  lifetime following scientology's belief  system was much more difficult you leave  in 2007 but you still hold on to the  teachings when did you finally reach a  point where like oh my goodness what was  I in the grips of it took about six  years six years to what to come to that  conclusion that no this is a load of  crap it's very difficult  to accept the fact that you have been so  stupid I don't think that you can take  Mike render at his word when he's  answered your questions in the interview  one thing Mike rinder and Monique  Yingling do agree on the Church of  scientology's vast wealth when I left in  2007 I knew that between the two major  arms of Scientology that accumulated  about two billion dollars in money and  assets that has increased a lot since  then what's the church worth  foreign  I can't give you a dollar figure but as  I said a bit earlier the church does  have a very generous parishioners well  hang on you can't give me a dollar  figure but you're one of the lead  attorneys in dealing with all the tax  issues you must have a sense of the net  worth of the Church of Scientology  well the church does have  hundreds of millions billions well the  real question is what are what does the  church use its assets for and it uses  its assets to afford its religious and  charitable Mission am I accurate in  describing the assets of the church in  billions probably yes all of that money  raised from its millions of members real  estate in Prime locations is tax-free as  the IRS granted Scientology the same tax  exempt status as other major religions  in 1993 people are watching Mike rinder  what do you want people to know who are  watching him  share his view of what he says his  experience was I think your your  listeners need to know that he is paid  for attacking the church that this is  Money Motivated Mike renders only source  of income  for the last 15 years or so has been  based on his being a Critic of the  church rinder denies those claims  telling CBS News my criticism of  Scientology is driven solely by my  desire to end the rampant ongoing abuses  within the organization it is  Scientology that is obsessed with making  money you know people are going to watch  this and be like hang on a second this  guy was the spokesman for the Church of  Scientology you admitted to me in the  interviews you told a lot of lies so why  are we supposed to believe what you're  telling us about scientology now I can't  convince you one way or the other  whether I am lying now or then or both  you just got to sort of  look at me and go is there a reason for  him to lie now there is no reason for me  to lie now the only thing that I am  trying to accomplish is stop people from  getting  abused by Scientology  today rinder lives in Florida not so far  from Scientology spiritual headquarters  in Clearwater when rinder left  Scientology he left behind a wife and  two children wow this book he says is  dedicated to his kids who he hopes will  one day leave the organization in  response to the book rinder's children  wrote an open letter to rinder calling  the Memoir self-serving they wrote quote  you were not our father stop telling  lies about us your family our friends  and our beliefs  hmm whoa doesn't sound a lot to um yeah  reconciliation imminent by any means had  to be very painful though to hear those  words coming from your own children it  was and he says look all I can do is  make the invitation he makes it clear in  the in the book that he is hoping to  leave the door open but clearly from the  response that door is going to remain  closed for quite a while so what's he  doing now so he lives actually not too  far from the church headquarters he has  a another wife and a new family she too  is a former Scientologist wow really  powerful interview Jim yeah thank you  thanks well done thank you sir

